{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576494357,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU1MzgzNDE4MTY=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3929","origin":"github"},"title":"'git annex drop' on NFS mount doesn't free up space","message":"Demo:\n\n```\nmkdir nfsdrop\ncd nfsdrop\ngit init\ngit annex init\necho 123 \u003e file1\ngit annex add file1\ngit commit -m dummy\ngit annex drop --force file1\n```\n\nwhich reliably gives me something like (across different machines and NFS setups)\n\n```\n% tree -a .git/annex/objects\n.git/annex/objects\n└── G6\n    └── qW\n        └── SHA256E-s4--181210f8f9c779c26da1d9b2075bde0127302ee0e3fca38c9a83f5b1dd8e5d3b\n            └── .nfs0000000000141e900001531a\n\n% cat .git/annex/objects/G6/qW/SHA256E-s4--181210f8f9c779c26da1d9b2075bde0127302ee0e3fca38c9a83f5b1dd8e5d3b/.nfs0000000000141e900001531a \n123\n```\n\nSo in essence, git-annex has dropped the file (repo integrity is fine), but effectively no data has left the system. This was discovered in https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3921 where a drop was supposed to prevent the disk from filling up, but did not.","files":null}]}